So we found that $h'(x)=\dfrac{1}{4}x^{^{\Large-\frac{3}{4}}}$, which can also be written as $\dfrac{1}{4(\sqrt[4]{x})^3}$

Now let's plug $\blueD{x=16}$:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{1}{4(\sqrt[4]{\blueD{16}})^3}&=\dfrac{1}{4(2)^3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{4\cdot 8}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{32}
\end{align}$
